1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,180
{\an8}PALLISTER AND DUNN

2
00:00:07,491 --> 00:00:09,152
[gulls over an empty promenade]

3
00:00:16,656 --> 00:00:20,109
- Two minutes, Dot.
- I heard you the first time.

4
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:24,556
There is a man in Menswear
deciding about a cardigan.

5
00:00:27,712 --> 00:00:31,011
He has been deciding about it since four o'clock.

6
00:00:35,596 --> 00:00:39,493
- Shall I go and stand near him?
- Stand near him kindly.

7
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:44,320
<i>Winnie? Is it ten o'clock yet?</i>

8
00:00:45,506 --> 00:00:46,506
Not yet, Gerald.

9
00:00:47,615 --> 00:00:49,082
<i>It feels like ten o'clock.</i>

10
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,057
It often does.

11
00:00:59,471 --> 00:01:02,932
- We're closing now, sir.
- I'll take the green one.

12
00:01:03,592 --> 00:01:07,614
The green one is the same cardigan
as the blue one, in green.

13
00:01:13,537 --> 00:01:16,654
- Then I'll have the blue.
- Very good, sir.

14
00:01:17,564 --> 00:01:19,189
Thank you, sir. Mind the step.

15
00:01:24,857 --> 00:01:27,192
Forty-one years I have worked here.

16
00:01:28,126 --> 00:01:30,639
- And?
- And nobody ever takes the green.

17
00:01:36,692 --> 00:01:38,502
- Doors, please.
- Doors.

18
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:40,655
[the shutters coming down]

19
00:01:41,157 --> 00:01:42,841
That is the shop shut, then.

20
00:01:44,012 --> 00:01:45,780
- For tonight.
- For tonight.

21
00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:56,501
Night staff to the tannoy, please.

22
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,753
- Evening, Winnie.
- Evening, Gerald.

23
00:02:03,418 --> 00:02:06,372
- I thought I'd say a few words.
- There's no rush.

24
00:02:11,126 --> 00:02:12,228
Where is the boy?

25
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:14,827
<i>Winnie, it's Mrs. Ayres.</i>

26
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:20,834
Go ahead, Mrs. Ayres.

27
00:02:21,666 --> 00:02:23,704
<i>The back stairs are locked at ten.</i>

28
00:02:24,642 --> 00:02:26,669
<i>And the loading bay door sticks.</i>

29
00:02:27,307 --> 00:02:28,454
It has always stuck.

30
00:02:28,968 --> 00:02:31,053
<i>I'm only saying what it does.</i>

31
00:02:40,918 --> 00:02:42,516
[the back stairs door banging]

32
00:02:43,335 --> 00:02:46,347
- Sorry. Sorry. The bus.
- You are nine minutes early.

33
00:02:47,149 --> 00:02:48,821
- Am I?
- You are. Stand there.

34
00:02:49,955 --> 00:02:52,724
Those are not shoes for a stock-take.

35
00:02:53,555 --> 00:02:56,724
- They're my best trainers.
- That is the trouble.

36
00:02:57,418 --> 00:02:59,737
You will be standing until six.

37
00:03:03,276 --> 00:03:06,742
- I've got thick socks.
- Thick socks are not shoes.

38
00:03:07,146 --> 00:03:10,914
- Is there anything in Lost Property?
- There is. None of it fits.

39
00:03:11,447 --> 00:03:13,571
There are plimsolls under my counter.

40
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:16,753
- Whose are they?
- Mine. From 1994.

41
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:21,641
- Do I have to?
- You have to.

42
00:03:31,717 --> 00:03:33,528
Stock sheets. One floor each.

43
00:03:37,962 --> 00:03:40,064
- Thank you.
- Thank you, Winnie.

44
00:03:42,481 --> 00:03:44,163
Top to bottom, left to right.

45
00:03:45,048 --> 00:03:46,887
Front to back. Nothing skipped.

46
00:03:47,877 --> 00:03:50,223
Nothing guessed. Write it in ink.

47
00:03:51,048 --> 00:03:55,637
- What if I get it wrong?
- Then you will have written it wrong.

48
00:03:56,092 --> 00:03:58,064
Count it twice, say it once.

49
00:03:59,247 --> 00:04:02,424
- Count it twice?
- The second count is the real one.

50
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,669
- Should I say that on the tannoy?
- No.

51
00:04:07,152 --> 00:04:10,198
Kyle, you have Bay Twelve.
Soft Furnishings, first floor.

52
00:04:12,525 --> 00:04:17,719
- Which one is Bay Twelve?
- The one with the label on it saying twelve.

53
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:19,815
{\an8}BAY TWELVE - SOFT FURNISHINGS

54
00:04:20,284 --> 00:04:21,286
- Found it.
- Good.

55
00:04:30,095 --> 00:04:32,319
Dot, Haberdashery and the mezzanine.

56
00:04:36,801 --> 00:04:38,384
Haberdashery is done.

57
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:41,919
- Done?
- Done at four. It was quiet.

58
00:04:42,681 --> 00:04:45,063
You counted it in trading hours.

59
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:47,867
- Was that wrong?
- It was early.

60
00:04:48,836 --> 00:04:51,636
Nine thousand four hundred and six buttons.

61
00:04:52,416 --> 00:04:54,537
- Six?
- Six. I counted them twice.

62
00:04:59,543 --> 00:05:01,510
- And I said it once.
- Good.

63
00:05:01,965 --> 00:05:05,296
- How do you know where it all is?
- I have always known.

64
00:05:11,508 --> 00:05:13,152
[the tannoy clicking on]

65
00:05:14,131 --> 00:05:15,430
<i>Good evening, everyone.</i>

66
00:05:16,453 --> 00:05:18,291
<i>This is Gerald, from the office.</i>

67
00:05:18,801 --> 00:05:21,082
<i>As you know, tonight is the last</i>

68
00:05:22,184 --> 00:05:23,667
<i>stock-take before the sale.</i>

69
00:05:24,413 --> 00:05:26,578
<i>I won't keep you. Count carefully,</i>

70
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:29,128
<i>and remember why we are all here.</i>

71
00:05:30,094 --> 00:05:31,359
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

72
00:05:37,522 --> 00:05:40,337
- What did that mean?
- Nothing. It was lovely.

73
00:05:41,462 --> 00:05:42,726
He does one every year.

74
00:05:43,329 --> 00:05:46,036
He did one at his grandfather's funeral.

75
00:05:47,151 --> 00:05:49,881
- On the tannoy?
- There was a tannoy.

76
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:52,422
There are four of us, Gerald.

77
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:55,162
- I know.
- We could all hear you.

78
00:06:06,244 --> 00:06:08,175
The grotto has to come down tonight.

79
00:06:09,265 --> 00:06:12,005
- It's September.
- It has been up since November.

80
00:06:12,882 --> 00:06:14,865
Why is there still a grotto?

81
00:06:15,318 --> 00:06:17,372
Nobody could agree whose job it was.

82
00:06:18,492 --> 00:06:20,760
It goes down to the loading bay.

83
00:06:21,742 --> 00:06:24,048
Reindeer first. It's the big one.

84
00:06:28,609 --> 00:06:31,666
- How many reindeer are there?
- Nine. There is always a ninth.

85
00:06:32,865 --> 00:06:34,400
It's heavier than it looks.

86
00:06:35,208 --> 00:06:38,445
- Mind the doorway.
- I am minding the doorway.

87
00:06:38,964 --> 00:06:41,120
<i>Are we moving the sleigh as well?</i>

88
00:06:41,748 --> 00:06:42,849
There is no sleigh.

89
00:06:49,047 --> 00:06:50,063
<i>There was a sleigh.</i>

90
00:06:50,497 --> 00:06:52,323
There was a sleigh in 1988.

91
00:07:04,222 --> 00:07:06,957
- Antlers in first.
- Antlers in first, yes.

92
00:07:09,104 --> 00:07:11,462
It won't go in with the head up.

93
00:07:12,055 --> 00:07:14,677
- Then put the head down.
- It doesn't bend.

94
00:07:15,486 --> 00:07:17,880
Gerald, don't send it up on its own.

95
00:07:22,935 --> 00:07:23,935
I've sent it.

96
00:07:24,578 --> 00:07:27,757
- Is it meant to make that noise?
- It is not.

97
00:07:29,879 --> 00:07:30,879
It has stopped.

98
00:07:36,076 --> 00:07:38,940
Between the mezzanine
and the first floor.

99
00:07:43,045 --> 00:07:45,312
- Can you see it?
- I can see one antler.

100
00:07:57,984 --> 00:08:00,458
Mrs. Ayres, the goods lift has stopped.

101
00:08:01,307 --> 00:08:02,307
<i>Stopped where?</i>

102
00:08:02,752 --> 00:08:05,461
Between two floors. There's a reindeer in it.

103
00:08:05,924 --> 00:08:06,924
<i>Say again?</i>

104
00:08:12,768 --> 00:08:14,515
The reindeer from the grotto.

105
00:08:15,585 --> 00:08:17,543
<i>Right. I'll put it in the book.</i>

106
00:08:18,391 --> 00:08:20,746
- Is there a book?
- There is a book.

107
00:08:21,396 --> 00:08:23,296
Pressing it more will not help.

108
00:08:24,191 --> 00:08:27,444
- I'm pressing it differently.
- It isn't a piano.

109
00:08:28,448 --> 00:08:30,304
I'll ring the lift company.

110
00:08:30,756 --> 00:08:32,503
It's half past ten at night.

111
00:08:33,274 --> 00:08:36,883
- They have an emergency line.
- Everyone has an emergency line.

112
00:08:37,625 --> 00:08:39,804
<i>Thank you for calling. The office</i>

113
00:08:40,437 --> 00:08:42,151
<i>is closed until eight tomorrow.</i>

114
00:08:43,175 --> 00:08:45,297
- After eight what?
- I didn't ask.

115
00:08:46,185 --> 00:08:48,172
Then it stays where it is.

116
00:08:48,627 --> 00:08:49,682
We count around it.

117
00:08:50,216 --> 00:08:52,009
- All night?
- All night.

118
00:09:04,489 --> 00:09:06,029
Four floors. Eight hours.

119
00:09:07,139 --> 00:09:08,853
One lift we are not using.

120
00:09:09,571 --> 00:09:11,587
Count it twice, say it once.

121
00:09:12,731 --> 00:09:14,182
- She means it.
- I know.

122
00:09:15,113 --> 00:09:17,906
- Is it still humming?
- It does that.

123
00:09:18,729 --> 00:09:20,680
- Will it stop?
- Everything stops.

124
00:09:21,265 --> 00:09:22,450
Right. Stock sheets.

125
00:09:23,271 --> 00:09:24,745
[the goods lift going quiet]

126
00:09:32,204 --> 00:09:33,362
Haberdashery is still done.

127
00:09:34,215 --> 00:09:35,923
- Since four?
- Since four. I did say.

128
00:09:36,730 --> 00:09:38,596
I have been letting you enjoy it.

129
00:09:39,377 --> 00:09:41,080
- Totals?
- All of them.

130
00:09:42,208 --> 00:09:45,203
- Buttons?
- By the gross, by the card, and by the jar.

131
00:09:46,227 --> 00:09:47,227
Zips.

132
00:09:48,409 --> 00:09:49,855
Three hundred and eighty.

133
00:09:50,296 --> 00:09:52,331
Count it twice, say it once.

134
00:09:52,932 --> 00:09:55,117
I counted these in 1987 as well.

135
00:09:59,794 --> 00:10:01,847
They have not moved much since.

136
00:10:02,991 --> 00:10:05,824
- Ribbon?
- Two hundred and nine metres.

137
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:07,440
Thread.

138
00:10:08,566 --> 00:10:11,838
I stopped saying thread out loud in about 1994.

139
00:10:12,786 --> 00:10:13,786
Why?

140
00:10:14,571 --> 00:10:16,370
Nobody was writing it down.

141
00:10:29,566 --> 00:10:30,917
[the radio crackling]

142
00:10:31,876 --> 00:10:33,225
<i>Winnie. It's Mrs. Ayres.</i>

143
00:10:33,780 --> 00:10:35,522
<i>A door's open on the loading bay.</i>

144
00:10:36,488 --> 00:10:39,506
- Which door?
- That's rather the point.

145
00:10:40,006 --> 00:10:41,700
<i>I've not opened anything myself.</i>

146
00:10:42,593 --> 00:10:43,598
Noted, Mrs. Ayres.

147
00:10:44,533 --> 00:10:46,397
- You said noted.
- I did.

148
00:10:46,893 --> 00:10:49,342
Noted is what you say to Gerald.

149
00:10:59,537 --> 00:11:00,842
Winnie. Small problem.

150
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:02,360
How small?

151
00:11:03,558 --> 00:11:04,558
Curtains.

152
00:11:05,156 --> 00:11:06,858
There are two sorts of curtain.

153
00:11:07,985 --> 00:11:09,900
There are eleven sorts of curtain.

154
00:11:13,203 --> 00:11:15,085
Right. That is broadly the problem.

155
00:11:16,043 --> 00:11:18,285
The sheet has one box for curtains.

156
00:11:18,717 --> 00:11:21,148
The sheet has a box for each line code.

157
00:11:24,357 --> 00:11:27,058
- Did you turn the page?
- There's a second page?

158
00:11:27,904 --> 00:11:29,318
I turned mine over.

159
00:11:30,383 --> 00:11:31,383
Good lad.

160
00:11:32,012 --> 00:11:33,012
Twice.

161
00:11:33,989 --> 00:11:34,989
Less good.

162
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:38,204
- So I start again.
- You start again.

163
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,625
[a lift motor straining, then giving up]

164
00:11:50,638 --> 00:11:52,820
- Still stuck, then.
- Still stuck.

165
00:11:53,584 --> 00:11:55,608
Between the mezzanine and first floor.

166
00:11:56,415 --> 00:11:58,266
With the grotto reindeer inside it.

167
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:00,696
- All of them?
- Eight.

168
00:12:01,326 --> 00:12:02,326
There were nine.

169
00:12:07,925 --> 00:12:09,261
One is in Lost Property.

170
00:12:09,906 --> 00:12:13,057
How does a reindeer get to
Lost Property?

171
00:12:13,668 --> 00:12:14,723
Somebody lost it.

172
00:12:15,747 --> 00:12:18,145
Has anyone rung Terry at the lift people?

173
00:12:19,292 --> 00:12:20,562
- You have.
- Have I?

174
00:12:25,233 --> 00:12:26,274
You are about to.

175
00:12:36,806 --> 00:12:39,271
<i>Larchmont Lift Services.
The office is closed.</i>

176
00:12:40,436 --> 00:12:42,435
<i>Our hours are nine until five.</i>

177
00:12:42,955 --> 00:12:44,548
<i>Please speak after the tone.</i>

178
00:12:45,164 --> 00:12:46,746
[an answering machine beep]

179
00:12:47,815 --> 00:12:49,435
Hello. Gerald Pallister here.

180
00:12:55,126 --> 00:12:57,024
Pallister and Dunn, Marchbank.

181
00:12:58,090 --> 00:12:59,862
Our goods lift has stopped.

182
00:13:00,710 --> 00:13:02,365
There are reindeer in it.

183
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:04,180
Not live ones.

184
00:13:10,113 --> 00:13:11,799
I should have opened with that.

185
00:13:12,412 --> 00:13:13,896
Thank you. Sorry. Goodbye.

186
00:13:14,693 --> 00:13:17,597
- Did they answer?
- In a manner of speaking.

187
00:13:29,078 --> 00:13:30,879
- Kyle.
- Yes, Mrs. Cadogan.

188
00:13:31,851 --> 00:13:33,587
- Winnie.
- Yes, Winnie Cadogan.

189
00:13:34,596 --> 00:13:36,315
Lost Property. Count it.

190
00:13:37,479 --> 00:13:40,048
- All of it?
- It's a cupboard, not a wing.

191
00:13:40,951 --> 00:13:42,669
What if a thing isn't a thing?

192
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:44,923
Everything is a thing.

193
00:13:45,371 --> 00:13:46,937
Count it twice, say it once.

194
00:13:52,473 --> 00:13:53,590
- Twice.
- Twice.

195
00:14:00,839 --> 00:14:02,863
{\an8}LOST PROPERTY - ENQUIRE AT TILL 4

196
00:14:03,543 --> 00:14:04,543
One glove.

197
00:14:04,973 --> 00:14:06,609
One other glove. Not a pair.

198
00:14:10,266 --> 00:14:11,266
One set of teeth.

199
00:14:14,567 --> 00:14:15,567
One reindeer.

200
00:14:16,686 --> 00:14:18,012
[a clatter of umbrellas]

201
00:14:18,784 --> 00:14:19,784
Oh.

202
00:14:32,471 --> 00:14:34,226
<i>Good evening, second floor.</i>

203
00:14:34,988 --> 00:14:36,796
<i>Gerald here. Nothing urgent.</i>

204
00:14:37,695 --> 00:14:39,668
<i>My pink pen has gone missing.</i>

205
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:42,870
<i>It's the pen I do the sheets with.</i>

206
00:14:43,730 --> 00:14:44,996
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

207
00:14:47,297 --> 00:14:49,393
- Where is it?
- Behind his ear.

208
00:14:50,398 --> 00:14:52,041
It is always behind his ear.

209
00:14:53,151 --> 00:14:56,976
- Should we tell him?
- He'll find it when he writes.

210
00:15:07,545 --> 00:15:08,545
- Dot.
- Winnie.

211
00:15:09,071 --> 00:15:10,071
Soft Furnishings.

212
00:15:10,841 --> 00:15:11,841
Is a department.

213
00:15:12,602 --> 00:15:14,230
It is nine hours of cushions.

214
00:15:14,735 --> 00:15:16,987
It is seven if you do it properly.

215
00:15:17,392 --> 00:15:18,726
Then do it properly.

216
00:15:23,986 --> 00:15:25,438
I have done my department.

217
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:33,083
I have done this department every year since 1985.

218
00:15:34,187 --> 00:15:35,934
- This is the last one.
- I know.

219
00:15:36,750 --> 00:15:39,068
I would like mine to stay done.

220
00:15:39,605 --> 00:15:41,659
Nobody will touch your drawers.

221
00:15:45,081 --> 00:15:47,538
Somebody always touches the drawers.

222
00:15:50,610 --> 00:15:53,656
- I'll put a notice on them.
- A notice.

223
00:15:55,963 --> 00:15:58,249
- Gerald reads notices out loud.
- He does.

224
00:15:59,068 --> 00:16:01,097
And then does the opposite.

225
00:16:02,189 --> 00:16:05,575
- Two hours on Soft Furnishings.
- Ninety minutes.

226
00:16:06,197 --> 00:16:07,648
Then sit down until six.

227
00:16:11,727 --> 00:16:12,727
With tea.

228
00:16:13,750 --> 00:16:15,376
There is no tea until two.

229
00:16:16,251 --> 00:16:18,110
There is tea in Haberdashery.

230
00:16:23,218 --> 00:16:24,669
- Since when?
- Since 1985.

231
00:16:25,739 --> 00:16:26,739
Ninety minutes.

232
00:16:27,659 --> 00:16:28,659
Done.

233
00:16:39,673 --> 00:16:41,099
<i>Winnie. Mrs. Ayres again.</i>

234
00:16:42,163 --> 00:16:44,206
<i>That loading bay door is still open.</i>

235
00:16:48,223 --> 00:16:49,912
- How many so far?
- Sorry?

236
00:16:50,444 --> 00:16:51,444
Umbrellas.

237
00:17:02,605 --> 00:17:03,605
Thirty-eight.

238
00:17:04,395 --> 00:17:05,395
Thirty-nine.

239
00:17:06,547 --> 00:17:07,547
Forty.

240
00:17:08,466 --> 00:17:09,914
[rain on the skylight]

241
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:14,900
Hold on.

242
00:17:16,065 --> 00:17:17,944
There's one behind the coats.

243
00:17:19,070 --> 00:17:22,061
- Was that one in the stand?
- It was not in the stand.

244
00:17:22,619 --> 00:17:23,962
Forty-one umbrellas.

245
00:17:33,252 --> 00:17:35,660
Forty-one umbrellas in that cupboard.

246
00:17:40,763 --> 00:17:43,487
- Forty-one. Same as my years.
- I know.

247
00:17:44,171 --> 00:17:46,332
Nobody ever came back for one of them.

248
00:17:47,333 --> 00:17:49,417
People put a thing down and they go.

249
00:17:50,261 --> 00:17:52,378
This isn't a stock-take, Winnie.

250
00:17:53,082 --> 00:17:54,759
It's a list of what we had.

251
00:18:07,507 --> 00:18:08,890
[strip light buzzes]

252
00:18:11,915 --> 00:18:15,046
- Kyle. Read me the top line.
- Bay Twelve.

253
00:18:15,957 --> 00:18:16,957
And the figure.

254
00:18:19,592 --> 00:18:20,592
Four hundred.

255
00:18:21,270 --> 00:18:24,257
- Four hundred what?
- It doesn't say what.

256
00:18:24,665 --> 00:18:28,121
{\an8}BAY TWELVE - TROUSER PRESSES
STOCK SHEET FIGURE: 400

257
00:18:28,693 --> 00:18:29,693
Trouser presses.

258
00:18:30,176 --> 00:18:33,174
- Sorry?
- Trouser presses. Four hundred.

259
00:18:33,652 --> 00:18:34,652
In Marchbank.

260
00:18:35,184 --> 00:18:36,184
Apparently.

261
00:18:36,937 --> 00:18:39,238
Who buys a trouser press at the seaside?

262
00:18:39,928 --> 00:18:41,751
Somebody with damp trousers.

263
00:18:42,553 --> 00:18:45,252
- Is that a real answer?
- It is at half eleven.

264
00:18:50,390 --> 00:18:52,437
Wall end. Work in. Two to a box.

265
00:19:00,305 --> 00:19:02,592
<i>Winnie. Dot. On the back stairs.</i>

266
00:19:03,752 --> 00:19:05,806
<i>The goods lift hasn't moved.</i>

267
00:19:06,296 --> 00:19:09,347
- Which is where?
- Between the mezzanine and one. Sulking.

268
00:19:10,201 --> 00:19:11,201
Take your time.

269
00:19:12,315 --> 00:19:14,196
<i>Forty-one years. One more minute.</i>

270
00:19:15,068 --> 00:19:16,068
[radio crackles]

271
00:19:16,684 --> 00:19:18,664
<i>Mrs. Ayres. Loading bay's locked.</i>

272
00:19:19,371 --> 00:19:20,371
Thank you.

273
00:19:21,007 --> 00:19:23,044
<i>Nothing in or out since ten.</i>

274
00:19:23,724 --> 00:19:24,724
Noted.

275
00:19:36,014 --> 00:19:38,690
- How many have you got?
- Eighty-one.

276
00:19:39,737 --> 00:19:40,737
Eighty-one what?

277
00:19:41,493 --> 00:19:42,493
Presses.

278
00:19:43,211 --> 00:19:44,420
Boxes or presses?

279
00:19:45,110 --> 00:19:48,622
- Is there a difference?
- Two to a box, Kyle.

280
00:19:49,306 --> 00:19:51,119
Then a hundred and sixty-two.

281
00:19:51,822 --> 00:19:52,822
Or eighty-one.

282
00:19:53,413 --> 00:19:54,413
Start again.

283
00:19:55,134 --> 00:19:57,026
- From eighty-one?
- From nothing.

284
00:20:02,974 --> 00:20:03,989
[telephone ringing]

285
00:20:05,103 --> 00:20:06,457
<i>Lift services. Terry.</i>

286
00:20:10,772 --> 00:20:13,009
Terry. Gerald Pallister. Marchbank.

287
00:20:14,085 --> 00:20:15,425
<i>The department store.</i>

288
00:20:15,973 --> 00:20:17,597
The department store, yes.

289
00:20:18,792 --> 00:20:19,792
<i>Goods lift?</i>

290
00:20:20,302 --> 00:20:21,832
Stuck between the mezzanine and one.

291
00:20:22,454 --> 00:20:23,699
<i>Doors open or shut?</i>

292
00:20:24,418 --> 00:20:25,418
Shut.

293
00:20:27,894 --> 00:20:29,266
<i>Anybody inside it?</i>

294
00:20:30,448 --> 00:20:31,448
A trolley.

295
00:20:31,970 --> 00:20:33,773
<i>Then it isn't an emergency.</i>

296
00:20:34,482 --> 00:20:36,431
It is from where I'm standing.

297
00:20:41,702 --> 00:20:43,161
<i>I'm twenty minutes away.</i>

298
00:20:44,145 --> 00:20:45,145
Twenty minutes.

299
00:20:52,056 --> 00:20:54,212
- Well?
- Terry says twenty minutes.

300
00:20:54,671 --> 00:20:55,671
Right.

301
00:20:56,338 --> 00:20:57,874
Fenella gets here at two.

302
00:21:03,745 --> 00:21:05,437
- She does.
- With a folder.

303
00:21:09,556 --> 00:21:10,556
Two hours, then.

304
00:21:21,140 --> 00:21:23,126
Four hundred and seventeen.

305
00:21:23,844 --> 00:21:25,943
The stock sheets say four hundred.

306
00:21:26,479 --> 00:21:28,617
I know what the stock sheets say.

307
00:21:29,094 --> 00:21:31,201
So where did seventeen come from?

308
00:21:31,722 --> 00:21:34,820
Nowhere. Seventeen of them came from nowhere.

309
00:21:38,135 --> 00:21:39,893
Could a delivery have come in?

310
00:21:43,598 --> 00:21:45,697
Nothing comes in. Things only leave.

311
00:21:46,642 --> 00:21:47,642
Slowly.

312
00:21:48,046 --> 00:21:49,883
Count it twice, say it once.

313
00:21:51,030 --> 00:21:52,814
- We have counted it.
- Once.

314
00:21:53,893 --> 00:21:54,893
Again. Wall end.

315
00:22:03,782 --> 00:22:05,400
Kyle. What's that behind you.

316
00:22:05,801 --> 00:22:07,028
It was in the cupboard.

317
00:22:12,404 --> 00:22:13,853
[umbrella clatters on tile]

318
00:22:17,223 --> 00:22:19,923
- Another one?
- That's the cupboard empty.

319
00:22:20,426 --> 00:22:21,639
Do we count umbrellas?

320
00:22:22,405 --> 00:22:25,276
We count everything that is in the building.

321
00:22:25,972 --> 00:22:27,884
Even what nobody came back for.

322
00:22:29,082 --> 00:22:30,230
Especially that.

323
00:22:30,800 --> 00:22:31,800
Forty-one.

324
00:22:32,528 --> 00:22:33,762
Forty-two umbrellas.

325
00:22:41,472 --> 00:22:42,998
Gerald. Step away from it.

326
00:22:43,782 --> 00:22:46,252
- I'm going to say something.
- To whom?

327
00:22:47,298 --> 00:22:48,298
To everyone.

328
00:22:53,872 --> 00:22:55,937
There are four of us and a radio.

329
00:22:56,435 --> 00:22:57,867
[distant tannoy chime]

330
00:22:58,598 --> 00:23:01,757
<i>Bay Twelve is being counted again.
Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

331
00:23:02,413 --> 00:23:04,669
- Was that useful?
- It was reassuring.

332
00:23:05,190 --> 00:23:06,190
To whom?

333
00:23:06,911 --> 00:23:07,911
<i>Not to me.</i>

334
00:23:15,998 --> 00:23:17,601
They came in on a Tuesday.

335
00:23:18,258 --> 00:23:20,286
- When?
- Nineteen ninety-four.

336
00:23:21,064 --> 00:23:23,433
Somebody ordered them for a hotel.

337
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:25,508
The hotel never opened.

338
00:23:26,393 --> 00:23:27,429
So we kept them.

339
00:23:28,221 --> 00:23:29,600
We sell nine a year.

340
00:23:30,597 --> 00:23:33,892
- Nine a year?
- Forty-four years to shift them.

341
00:23:39,375 --> 00:23:41,819
The building's gone in six weeks.

342
00:23:42,258 --> 00:23:44,967
Then they're somebody else's forty-four years.

343
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:53,203
<i>Winnie. Mrs. Ayres.</i>

344
00:23:53,734 --> 00:23:54,734
Go on.

345
00:23:55,396 --> 00:23:57,562
<i>A stack of them in the grotto.</i>

346
00:23:58,445 --> 00:23:59,625
A stack of what?

347
00:24:02,851 --> 00:24:04,819
<i>Them. The ones you're counting.</i>

348
00:24:05,656 --> 00:24:06,656
How many?

349
00:24:12,882 --> 00:24:14,581
<i>I don't do numbers. I do doors.</i>

350
00:24:15,684 --> 00:24:17,411
Kyle. Grotto. Third floor.

351
00:24:18,498 --> 00:24:21,300
- Which one's the grotto?
- Past Soft Furnishings.

352
00:24:21,986 --> 00:24:23,403
It's got a throne in it.

353
00:24:29,590 --> 00:24:30,590
In September?

354
00:24:31,163 --> 00:24:33,324
<i>Winnie. They're ironing boards.</i>

355
00:24:34,333 --> 00:24:36,015
- How many?
- A lot of them.

356
00:24:36,479 --> 00:24:38,397
Then they don't concern us.

357
00:24:50,657 --> 00:24:51,657
It's humming.

358
00:24:52,588 --> 00:24:54,243
It's been humming since ten.

359
00:24:54,893 --> 00:24:55,893
Is that good?

360
00:25:01,854 --> 00:25:03,357
Better than the other noise.

361
00:25:04,539 --> 00:25:07,033
- What other noise?
- You'd know it.

362
00:25:08,027 --> 00:25:11,605
- Should I ring him again?
- Terry says twenty minutes.

363
00:25:18,725 --> 00:25:19,725
Well?

364
00:25:20,212 --> 00:25:22,003
Four hundred and nineteen.

365
00:25:22,953 --> 00:25:25,979
- It was seventeen an hour ago.
- It was.

366
00:25:30,412 --> 00:25:33,776
Nothing has gone up and nothing has come in.

367
00:25:34,653 --> 00:25:35,973
<i>Nothing in or out. Still.</i>

368
00:25:36,915 --> 00:25:38,268
Dot. What did you get?

369
00:25:39,092 --> 00:25:40,497
Four hundred and nineteen.

370
00:25:41,650 --> 00:25:43,906
- You counted separately?
- Opposite ends.

371
00:25:44,430 --> 00:25:47,708
So we agree with each other
and not with the stock sheets.

372
00:25:48,260 --> 00:25:50,051
That's the worse of the two.

373
00:25:50,893 --> 00:25:53,476
- What do I write?
- Nothing. Not in pen.

374
00:25:54,668 --> 00:25:57,380
- We counted it twice.
- And it's still wrong.

375
00:25:58,163 --> 00:26:00,098
Count it twice, say it once.

376
00:26:13,144 --> 00:26:15,261
[a fire door shutting two floors down]

377
00:26:16,379 --> 00:26:17,799
<i>Winnie. Are you receiving?</i>

378
00:26:22,923 --> 00:26:24,097
Receiving, Mrs. Ayres.

379
00:26:24,741 --> 00:26:26,500
<i>Fourth floor's dark and behaving.</i>

380
00:26:28,819 --> 00:26:29,819
Anything in it?

381
00:26:35,538 --> 00:26:36,880
<i>A moth. I let it out.</i>

382
00:26:38,023 --> 00:26:41,667
- She let a moth out.
- She's had a night of it.

383
00:26:42,269 --> 00:26:43,269
It's gone quiet.

384
00:26:48,892 --> 00:26:51,164
It's half twelve. It does that.

385
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:54,446
I thought it would feel bigger at night.

386
00:27:04,401 --> 00:27:05,900
[a drawer going in and out]

387
00:27:07,002 --> 00:27:08,717
- How far are you?
- Ribbons.

388
00:27:12,646 --> 00:27:14,437
You've been on ribbons an hour.

389
00:27:15,477 --> 00:27:18,039
There are four hundred and six of them.

390
00:27:19,033 --> 00:27:21,143
Nobody is ever going to check.

391
00:27:25,721 --> 00:27:26,721
I will know.

392
00:27:27,437 --> 00:27:30,133
- Do you want a hand?
- I want the company.

393
00:27:30,558 --> 00:27:33,444
Ninety-one, not ninety.
There's one in the window.

394
00:27:45,061 --> 00:27:46,084
- Still?
- Still.

395
00:27:47,091 --> 00:27:48,961
It's moved an inch since eleven.

396
00:27:50,013 --> 00:27:51,494
That inch took it two hours.

397
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:54,288
<i>Hello? Yes. Pallister and Dunn.</i>

398
00:27:55,076 --> 00:27:56,820
<i>No. Marchbank. On the coast.</i>

399
00:27:57,385 --> 00:27:59,249
He's doing the postcode again.

400
00:27:59,712 --> 00:28:01,430
He always does the postcode.

401
00:28:09,226 --> 00:28:10,612
No, it isn't an emergency.

402
00:28:11,328 --> 00:28:13,598
It's a lift with its door open.

403
00:28:14,487 --> 00:28:15,531
<i>Is anybody in it?</i>

404
00:28:16,691 --> 00:28:17,691
Nobody's in it.

405
00:28:18,108 --> 00:28:19,645
<i>Then it isn't an emergency.</i>

406
00:28:20,706 --> 00:28:22,477
That's rather what I said.

407
00:28:23,657 --> 00:28:25,379
<i>Twenty minutes. Give or take.</i>

408
00:28:31,319 --> 00:28:33,002
Give or take what, exactly?

409
00:28:33,936 --> 00:28:35,679
[the receiver going down]

410
00:28:36,296 --> 00:28:37,296
Winnie?

411
00:28:42,026 --> 00:28:43,652
Terry says twenty minutes.

412
00:28:44,400 --> 00:28:46,360
- He said that at ten.
- He did.

413
00:28:54,527 --> 00:28:56,670
- What's in this cupboard?
- Everything.

414
00:28:57,084 --> 00:28:58,786
Forty years of everything.

415
00:28:59,672 --> 00:29:01,084
<i>Kyle? Bring the list down.</i>

416
00:29:01,822 --> 00:29:03,417
There's a bicycle pump.

417
00:29:03,823 --> 00:29:04,856
There's a wig.

418
00:29:10,642 --> 00:29:12,586
Leave the wig where it is.

419
00:29:15,124 --> 00:29:16,253
And another umbrella.

420
00:29:17,309 --> 00:29:19,653
- Where was that one?
- Behind the wig.

421
00:29:20,568 --> 00:29:21,933
Forty-three umbrellas.

422
00:29:31,982 --> 00:29:33,386
Fifteen minutes. Sit down.

423
00:29:34,414 --> 00:29:37,115
- Is that allowed?
- I've just allowed it.

424
00:29:37,609 --> 00:29:39,432
There's tea in the flask.

425
00:29:40,543 --> 00:29:42,261
It's been stewing since ten.

426
00:29:42,768 --> 00:29:43,821
What have you got?

427
00:29:44,513 --> 00:29:46,074
- Cheese and what?
- Cheese.

428
00:29:48,591 --> 00:29:50,172
Mine's got a note in it.

429
00:29:51,192 --> 00:29:52,698
- Who from?
- My mum.

430
00:29:53,246 --> 00:29:54,392
What does it say?

431
00:29:55,074 --> 00:29:56,663
"Ring me when you're home."

432
00:29:57,606 --> 00:29:58,878
That's nice, that.

433
00:30:02,098 --> 00:30:04,026
It's a bit much. I'm seventeen.

434
00:30:13,841 --> 00:30:16,067
You've been here longest, haven't you.

435
00:30:21,819 --> 00:30:22,819
Since 1974.

436
00:30:23,377 --> 00:30:27,324
- That's before my mum.
- It's before a lot of people.

437
00:30:28,041 --> 00:30:29,051
What was it like?

438
00:30:29,619 --> 00:30:31,943
Busy. Four lifts, all of them working.

439
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:34,734
A man on the door in a hat.

440
00:30:35,837 --> 00:30:37,609
Did you start in Haberdashery?

441
00:30:38,667 --> 00:30:40,545
I started on the stock sheets.

442
00:30:41,235 --> 00:30:44,540
They put the new ones on the counting in those days.

443
00:30:45,473 --> 00:30:49,025
- You can't break anything counting.
- Do you still like it?

444
00:30:49,818 --> 00:30:51,660
I like knowing where things are.

445
00:30:58,261 --> 00:31:00,416
{\an8}STAFF OUTING, MARCHBANK, 1974

446
00:31:00,838 --> 00:31:02,363
That's me on the end.

447
00:31:02,917 --> 00:31:06,278
- Who's that next to you?
- Bernard. Carpets.

448
00:31:07,116 --> 00:31:08,469
He taught me the sheets.

449
00:31:09,438 --> 00:31:11,052
Count what's there, he said.

450
00:31:12,038 --> 00:31:13,697
Not what ought to be there.

451
00:31:14,296 --> 00:31:15,387
Is he still about?

452
00:31:15,867 --> 00:31:17,373
No. He died in 1998.

453
00:31:17,950 --> 00:31:20,089
The year they put the escalator in.

454
00:31:20,999 --> 00:31:23,372
- I'm sorry.
- You didn't know him.

455
00:31:24,448 --> 00:31:25,448
Still.

456
00:31:29,146 --> 00:31:31,342
He'd have hated that escalator.

457
00:31:32,303 --> 00:31:34,408
He hated anything that moved on its own.

458
00:31:35,168 --> 00:31:36,168
Were you two...

459
00:31:37,105 --> 00:31:40,393
We got forty minutes together at dinner time.

460
00:31:41,399 --> 00:31:43,281
Twenty-four years of forty minutes.

461
00:31:43,999 --> 00:31:47,603
- That isn't very much.
- It was plenty. Eat your sandwich.

462
00:31:58,658 --> 00:32:00,576
Second floor's signed off.

463
00:32:01,473 --> 00:32:05,068
You've been holding that clipboard like a shield.

464
00:32:05,481 --> 00:32:06,481
Have I.

465
00:32:07,454 --> 00:32:08,742
All night. Say it.

466
00:32:09,450 --> 00:32:11,109
Nobody's being kept on.

467
00:32:14,129 --> 00:32:16,280
After the sale. Not one of us.

468
00:32:20,152 --> 00:32:22,669
- You're sure?
- I've known since August.

469
00:32:23,693 --> 00:32:25,921
They told you and you came in anyway.

470
00:32:26,738 --> 00:32:27,812
It's the stock-take.

471
00:32:28,833 --> 00:32:30,104
Does Gerald know?

472
00:32:30,634 --> 00:32:33,747
Gerald thinks there are jobs at the new place.

473
00:32:34,313 --> 00:32:36,526
- There is no new place.
- No.

474
00:32:37,672 --> 00:32:40,147
Kyle's put his hours down to March.

475
00:32:40,889 --> 00:32:41,889
I know he has.

476
00:32:42,779 --> 00:32:45,176
- Are you telling them?
- Not tonight.

477
00:32:45,725 --> 00:32:46,725
Why not?

478
00:32:50,759 --> 00:32:52,822
They'd count it differently.

479
00:32:53,752 --> 00:32:55,547
And then the count would be wrong.

480
00:32:56,101 --> 00:32:58,650
- That isn't the reason.
- No. It isn't.

481
00:33:03,790 --> 00:33:05,863
Don't tell them yet. Please.

482
00:33:06,899 --> 00:33:09,312
- You've never said please to me.
- I know.

483
00:33:09,870 --> 00:33:10,870
All right.

484
00:33:11,506 --> 00:33:12,506
Thank you.

485
00:33:23,827 --> 00:33:24,827
<i>Not one of us.</i>

486
00:33:25,747 --> 00:33:27,858
<i>Don't tell them yet. Please.</i>

487
00:33:28,630 --> 00:33:31,080
- You all right, Mr. Pallister?
- Fine. Yes.

488
00:33:37,140 --> 00:33:39,400
- Were you after something?
- No. Nothing.

489
00:33:40,008 --> 00:33:41,602
I was going to say something.

490
00:33:45,404 --> 00:33:46,997
- Go on, then.
- It's gone.

491
00:33:54,225 --> 00:33:55,882
[the tannoy clicking on]

492
00:33:56,873 --> 00:34:00,925
<i>Ignore that. I was testing the tannoy.
Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

493
00:34:01,651 --> 00:34:04,256
- What's he testing it for?
- Nothing.

494
00:34:05,024 --> 00:34:07,759
He does it when he's nothing to say.

495
00:34:11,623 --> 00:34:13,698
Let him. It's a long time till two.

496
00:34:26,478 --> 00:34:27,678
Right. Where were we?

497
00:34:28,818 --> 00:34:30,819
- Bay Twelve.
- Bay Twelve.

498
00:34:31,798 --> 00:34:33,580
Count it twice, say it once.

499
00:34:34,408 --> 00:34:36,285
<i>Winnie. Anything to report?</i>

500
00:34:36,854 --> 00:34:38,865
[footsteps going four different ways]

501
00:34:51,874 --> 00:34:55,844
- Ground floor is done to Bay Eleven.
- Then we are ahead.

502
00:34:59,230 --> 00:35:01,665
Ahead is a word that gets people caught.

503
00:35:02,212 --> 00:35:06,447
- Ribbon drawer. By colour or by width?
- By width. Colour is a fashion.

504
00:35:07,639 --> 00:35:08,656
[radio crackles]

505
00:35:09,124 --> 00:35:10,952
<i>Winnie. Are you receiving?</i>

506
00:35:12,018 --> 00:35:13,457
Receiving, Mrs. Ayres.

507
00:35:13,991 --> 00:35:16,390
<i>There is a car in the loading bay.</i>

508
00:35:17,186 --> 00:35:18,638
<i>Two of them. Engine off.</i>

509
00:35:19,816 --> 00:35:22,027
<i>Grey. The expensive sort of grey.</i>

510
00:35:23,207 --> 00:35:24,806
That will be Miss Dunn.

511
00:35:25,506 --> 00:35:27,500
Keep the shutter down until I say.

512
00:35:37,272 --> 00:35:39,186
[footsteps on the back stairs]

513
00:35:40,036 --> 00:35:41,698
Winnie. Winnie. Winnie.

514
00:35:42,275 --> 00:35:44,281
I heard you at the first one, Gerald.

515
00:35:45,128 --> 00:35:47,901
- Fenella is in the loading bay.
- I know.

516
00:35:48,592 --> 00:35:49,972
She has somebody with her.

517
00:35:54,034 --> 00:35:56,281
I know that as well. Breathe out.

518
00:35:59,985 --> 00:36:02,174
The lift is still sitting between floors.

519
00:36:03,197 --> 00:36:06,518
- Did you ring them again?
- I rang them at midnight.

520
00:36:07,221 --> 00:36:09,065
Terry says twenty minutes.

521
00:36:12,422 --> 00:36:15,406
- He said twenty minutes at eleven.
- He did.

522
00:36:16,196 --> 00:36:20,913
Then we have had two lots of twenty minutes
and one lift that has not moved.

523
00:36:21,433 --> 00:36:23,646
Nobody comes up here tonight.

524
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:28,601
- What do I say if she asks?
- Say the mezzanine is counted.

525
00:36:29,398 --> 00:36:30,707
It is not counted.

526
00:36:35,312 --> 00:36:37,637
Then count it while she is downstairs.

527
00:36:47,717 --> 00:36:49,769
Kyle. You are on the back stairs.

528
00:36:53,083 --> 00:36:56,288
- Doing what?
- Making the stairs look dull.

529
00:36:57,083 --> 00:37:01,059
- If she speaks to you, what do you say?
- Good evening, Miss Dunn.

530
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:06,750
- And then?
- And then nothing at all.

531
00:37:19,808 --> 00:37:20,977
[a shutter rolling up]

532
00:37:22,149 --> 00:37:25,539
- How do I look?
- Like a man who has been up since seven.

533
00:37:30,623 --> 00:37:33,835
- I have been up since seven.
- Yes. Do not lead with that.

534
00:37:34,394 --> 00:37:35,854
<i>Winnie, the shutter is up.</i>

535
00:37:41,769 --> 00:37:46,279
- Where do we walk them first?
- Anywhere that is finished.

536
00:37:58,031 --> 00:38:02,677
- Gerald. You look well.
- Fenella. You look like you have plans.

537
00:38:03,168 --> 00:38:05,124
I have forty minutes and a torch.

538
00:38:10,633 --> 00:38:13,349
- Is this him?
- He does not do introductions.

539
00:38:17,476 --> 00:38:19,539
This is Winnie Cadogan, deputy manager.

540
00:38:20,598 --> 00:38:22,304
- We have spoken.
- Twice.

541
00:38:22,854 --> 00:38:25,265
Three times. The third one was short.

542
00:38:35,882 --> 00:38:38,796
- Is the whole floor counted?
- To Bay Eleven.

543
00:38:39,598 --> 00:38:41,468
- And Bay Twelve?
- In hand.

544
00:38:42,111 --> 00:38:43,694
In hand is a lovely phrase.

545
00:38:44,671 --> 00:38:47,544
It has held the shop up for ninety years.

546
00:38:48,692 --> 00:38:50,495
{\an8}GOODS LIFT AND MEZZANINE THIS WAY

547
00:38:51,084 --> 00:38:55,252
- Dot. You are standing on the arrow.
- I am standing where I always stand.

548
00:38:59,454 --> 00:39:04,141
- You have never stood there before.
- Forty-one years gives you range.

549
00:39:09,881 --> 00:39:13,624
- It is smaller than I remember it.
- Everything is. You were nine.

550
00:39:14,369 --> 00:39:19,513
- Do you still keep the button drawers?
- Two hundred and twelve of them.

551
00:39:19,955 --> 00:39:21,021
- Counted?
- Twice.

552
00:39:22,008 --> 00:39:23,593
Count it twice, say it once.

553
00:39:24,606 --> 00:39:26,628
Is that a rule of some sort?

554
00:39:31,872 --> 00:39:33,699
- It is a habit.
- Right.

555
00:39:34,215 --> 00:39:37,552
- And through there?
- Through there is Bay Twelve.

556
00:39:38,124 --> 00:39:40,640
- May we look?
- You can see it from here.

557
00:39:41,332 --> 00:39:44,016
It looks like a bay nobody has touched.

558
00:39:44,756 --> 00:39:46,756
That is roughly the look, yes.

559
00:39:47,322 --> 00:39:49,131
[a box sliding off a shelf]

560
00:39:49,733 --> 00:39:52,278
- That will be the count arriving.
- Will it.

561
00:39:59,157 --> 00:40:03,539
- Good evening, Miss Dunn.
- Good evening. What are you counting?

562
00:40:04,315 --> 00:40:05,315
Nothing yet.

563
00:40:05,801 --> 00:40:07,657
<i>Winnie. She is on the back stairs.</i>

564
00:40:08,567 --> 00:40:10,051
Thank you, Mrs. Ayres.

565
00:40:17,019 --> 00:40:18,079
[telephone ringing]

566
00:40:18,712 --> 00:40:20,537
Gerald. Do not answer that.

567
00:40:21,330 --> 00:40:22,594
It might be a customer.

568
00:40:23,254 --> 00:40:24,847
It is two in the morning.

569
00:40:25,954 --> 00:40:28,418
Pallister and Dunn, good evening.

570
00:40:29,202 --> 00:40:31,100
<i>Is that the shop? It is Terry.</i>

571
00:40:36,812 --> 00:40:38,735
<i>Only, is the lift still stuck?</i>

572
00:40:39,663 --> 00:40:41,666
<i>Because that is a two-man job.</i>

573
00:40:42,709 --> 00:40:44,453
We are all very well, thank you.

574
00:40:45,129 --> 00:40:46,785
<i>Who is? I asked about the lift.</i>

575
00:40:47,355 --> 00:40:48,673
And to you. Goodbye.

576
00:40:49,629 --> 00:40:52,629
- What was that?
- Lift people. They ring at odd hours.

577
00:40:53,076 --> 00:40:55,388
- About the lift?
- About lifts generally.

578
00:41:04,866 --> 00:41:07,845
- Why is there a grotto?
- That is the grotto.

579
00:41:08,707 --> 00:41:11,022
- It is September.
- It goes up in October.

580
00:41:12,133 --> 00:41:13,955
It has not come down since.

581
00:41:18,883 --> 00:41:22,538
- Whose idea was the grotto?
- Your grandfather's.

582
00:41:23,713 --> 00:41:26,955
- A reindeer. In Soft Furnishings.
- There is.

583
00:41:27,435 --> 00:41:29,515
He is very good with the children.

584
00:41:30,277 --> 00:41:32,441
- It is September.
- He is patient.

585
00:41:43,882 --> 00:41:46,101
- And the mezzanine?
- Counted.

586
00:41:46,927 --> 00:41:49,628
- By whom?
- By Mr. Pallister. This evening.

587
00:41:50,151 --> 00:41:51,765
<i>Good morning, Pallister's.</i>

588
00:41:52,534 --> 00:41:54,671
<i>This is Mr. Pallister speaking.</i>

589
00:41:55,639 --> 00:41:57,438
<i>Winnie to the mezzanine, please.</i>

590
00:41:58,401 --> 00:42:01,592
<i>The goods lift is still jammed</i>
<i>and Terry has not rung back.</i>

591
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:04,018
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

592
00:42:05,077 --> 00:42:06,657
He does like the tannoy.

593
00:42:11,735 --> 00:42:13,553
He counted it very thoroughly.

594
00:42:21,805 --> 00:42:23,280
May I see the stock sheets?

595
00:42:24,164 --> 00:42:25,236
They are the sheets.

596
00:42:25,718 --> 00:42:27,803
This one has two sets of figures on it.

597
00:42:28,542 --> 00:42:31,306
The first is the count. The second is the check.

598
00:42:31,805 --> 00:42:34,891
- Everything gets done twice?
- Everything that matters.

599
00:42:37,372 --> 00:42:40,268
- Who does the checking?
- Whoever is not tired.

600
00:42:40,703 --> 00:42:45,307
Nobody is on this floor at half two
unless they choose to be.

601
00:42:46,432 --> 00:42:48,438
They chose a long time ago.

602
00:42:49,326 --> 00:42:52,060
Your handwriting is on most of these.

603
00:42:57,501 --> 00:43:00,147
So is theirs. Look at the third column.

604
00:43:01,213 --> 00:43:02,998
- They initial it.
- Every page.

605
00:43:14,643 --> 00:43:17,195
Your buyer is looking at the time.

606
00:43:21,785 --> 00:43:24,653
He does that at the front door of everything.

607
00:43:25,137 --> 00:43:28,798
- He has seen four floors.
- He has seen three and a grotto.

608
00:43:31,056 --> 00:43:35,086
- Will he want the mezzanine?
- He will want the roof and the drains.

609
00:43:36,260 --> 00:43:38,746
Then he can come back in daylight.

610
00:43:39,849 --> 00:43:41,744
<i>The grey car is pulling out.</i>

611
00:43:42,521 --> 00:43:44,142
<i>Miss Dunn is not in it.</i>

612
00:43:47,329 --> 00:43:49,739
Kyle. Off the stairs. Sheets in the box.

613
00:43:50,728 --> 00:43:52,256
Count it twice, say it once.

614
00:43:52,759 --> 00:43:53,953
Yes, Mrs. Cadogan.

615
00:43:55,010 --> 00:43:56,752
<i>No. The shop is not the asset.</i>

616
00:43:57,225 --> 00:43:59,245
<i>It is the site. It always was.</i>

617
00:44:04,686 --> 00:44:06,460
<i>The name does not come with it.</i>

618
00:44:07,106 --> 00:44:08,496
Kyle, did you hear that?

619
00:44:09,412 --> 00:44:10,972
- Hear what?
- Good lad.

620
00:44:19,252 --> 00:44:20,492
You are still here.

621
00:44:21,046 --> 00:44:22,046
I am.

622
00:44:22,736 --> 00:44:25,167
- Your car has gone.
- It was his car.

623
00:44:30,991 --> 00:44:33,963
Tea is in Haberdashery. It is not good tea.

624
00:44:34,797 --> 00:44:36,351
I know. I have had it before.

625
00:44:36,813 --> 00:44:38,990
Then you know where the cups are.

626
00:44:46,151 --> 00:44:48,624
Half past two. Floors one and two are in.

627
00:45:00,767 --> 00:45:04,417
- Second floor is in as well.
- It isn't. Dot is still on the trims.

628
00:45:05,170 --> 00:45:07,103
Then we are waiting on Haberdashery.

629
00:45:10,104 --> 00:45:12,157
We are waiting on everything.

630
00:45:12,673 --> 00:45:14,646
[an adding machine rattles]

631
00:45:15,633 --> 00:45:18,048
Read me the counted total again, Fenella.

632
00:45:19,196 --> 00:45:20,783
Forty-one eight oh six.

633
00:45:24,228 --> 00:45:25,696
And the book figure.

634
00:45:26,425 --> 00:45:28,670
Forty-two nine four eight sixty.

635
00:45:29,672 --> 00:45:32,152
- Do it again.
- I have done it twice.

636
00:45:32,686 --> 00:45:34,566
Count it twice, say it once.

637
00:45:35,052 --> 00:45:36,807
Then I am saying it once.

638
00:45:41,479 --> 00:45:43,524
- How short?
- Eleven forty-two sixty.

639
00:45:46,595 --> 00:45:49,748
One thousand one hundred and forty-two pounds sixty.

640
00:45:50,240 --> 00:45:51,773
It is not a rounding error.

641
00:45:52,324 --> 00:45:53,880
{\an8}FLOOR 2 - SHORT 1,142.60

642
00:46:04,210 --> 00:46:08,146
- What is passementerie?
- Trimming. Expensive trimming.

643
00:46:09,290 --> 00:46:11,062
Shelf four. Count the reels.

644
00:46:14,584 --> 00:46:18,141
- Do I count the empty ones?
- You count what is there.

645
00:46:22,136 --> 00:46:23,805
Thirty-one of the gold.

646
00:46:24,956 --> 00:46:27,422
Write thirty-one. Then check it is thirty-one.

647
00:46:28,035 --> 00:46:30,196
I did the Lost Property sheet on my own.

648
00:46:31,117 --> 00:46:32,117
Yes. You did.

649
00:46:32,875 --> 00:46:34,231
Nobody came down to help.

650
00:46:35,360 --> 00:46:36,889
Nobody ever goes in there.

651
00:46:42,053 --> 00:46:43,597
[a two-way radio crackles]

652
00:46:49,033 --> 00:46:50,391
<i>Second floor, Mrs. Ayres.</i>

653
00:46:51,347 --> 00:46:52,955
Go ahead, Mrs. Ayres.

654
00:46:54,125 --> 00:46:55,894
<i>Is the boy still up there?</i>

655
00:46:57,030 --> 00:46:58,030
He is. Why?

656
00:46:58,530 --> 00:47:00,294
<i>No reason. Keeping track.</i>

657
00:47:01,257 --> 00:47:05,140
- What does she want?
- She keeps track. It is the job.

658
00:47:15,140 --> 00:47:17,272
- Is it done? Can we all go home?
- No.

659
00:47:17,819 --> 00:47:18,945
Sit down, Gerald.

660
00:47:19,694 --> 00:47:22,151
I have been counting the grotto.

661
00:47:22,700 --> 00:47:24,268
The second floor is short.

662
00:47:25,388 --> 00:47:28,758
- Short of what?
- Stock. Eleven forty-two sixty.

663
00:47:29,528 --> 00:47:31,816
- Is that pounds?
- That is pounds.

664
00:47:32,521 --> 00:47:33,521
Right.

665
00:47:33,948 --> 00:47:35,917
Who counted the second floor?

666
00:47:36,597 --> 00:47:38,874
Everybody counted the second floor.

667
00:47:43,922 --> 00:47:45,658
Somebody counted it first.

668
00:47:46,307 --> 00:47:48,209
Kyle did Lost Property on his own.

669
00:47:49,275 --> 00:47:50,405
Because I sent him.

670
00:47:51,522 --> 00:47:55,653
- Say what you mean, Gerald.
- I did not mean anything.

671
00:48:04,036 --> 00:48:05,600
[the goods lift shudders]

672
00:48:06,160 --> 00:48:09,398
- Is that Terry?
- That is the lift not being fixed.

673
00:48:10,176 --> 00:48:11,609
The floats are all correct.

674
00:48:12,127 --> 00:48:14,198
Then it is stock, not cash.

675
00:48:14,953 --> 00:48:17,100
It is a counting error until it isn't.

676
00:48:17,543 --> 00:48:21,409
- Should we ring somebody?
- Who would you ring at ten to three?

677
00:48:21,998 --> 00:48:24,252
I am going to say something helpful.

678
00:48:25,295 --> 00:48:27,742
- Gerald.
- It will take one minute.

679
00:48:28,409 --> 00:48:30,504
Nobody in this shop takes anything.

680
00:48:43,666 --> 00:48:44,851
[the tannoy clicks on]

681
00:48:49,052 --> 00:48:51,237
<i>Sorry to interrupt the counting.</i>

682
00:48:51,657 --> 00:48:53,822
<i>It is Gerald. Small announcement.</i>

683
00:48:54,597 --> 00:48:56,291
<i>The second floor count is short.</i>

684
00:49:02,810 --> 00:49:04,551
<i>By one thousand one hundred</i>

685
00:49:08,222 --> 00:49:10,143
<i>and forty-two pounds sixty.</i>

686
00:49:14,859 --> 00:49:15,859
Sit still.

687
00:49:16,781 --> 00:49:18,543
<i>Nobody is in any trouble at all.</i>

688
00:49:19,515 --> 00:49:20,594
<i>Especially not Kyle.</i>

689
00:49:21,573 --> 00:49:23,271
- He said my name.
- He did.

690
00:49:23,782 --> 00:49:25,886
<i>Kyle, that is not aimed at you.</i>

691
00:49:26,769 --> 00:49:28,381
<i>If anything has been moved,</i>

692
00:49:28,904 --> 00:49:30,927
<i>just put it back by the grotto.</i>

693
00:49:36,970 --> 00:49:38,981
<i>No questions will be asked.</i>

694
00:49:40,124 --> 00:49:41,506
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

695
00:49:42,088 --> 00:49:44,650
- Why did he say my name?
- Keep counting, love.

696
00:49:49,191 --> 00:49:50,191
I am counting.

697
00:49:51,013 --> 00:49:52,013
I know you are.

698
00:49:53,156 --> 00:49:54,156
Dot.

699
00:49:54,961 --> 00:49:58,615
Because he is a Pallister. They announce things.

700
00:50:06,114 --> 00:50:09,250
- That should settle everybody.
- Sit down, Gerald.

701
00:50:09,875 --> 00:50:10,875
You said his name.

702
00:50:11,742 --> 00:50:13,500
I said he was not in trouble.

703
00:50:18,736 --> 00:50:19,968
Then you said his name.

704
00:50:20,914 --> 00:50:21,914
Twice.

705
00:50:22,352 --> 00:50:23,874
It goes to all four floors.

706
00:50:24,668 --> 00:50:26,048
I know where it goes.

707
00:50:28,692 --> 00:50:31,266
There are four of us in the building.

708
00:50:31,816 --> 00:50:32,817
You used one name.

709
00:50:46,048 --> 00:50:47,458
- Kyle. Quick one.
- Yeah.

710
00:50:48,113 --> 00:50:49,652
Your Lost Property sheet.

711
00:50:50,439 --> 00:50:52,322
I gave it to Winnie at one.

712
00:50:53,520 --> 00:50:55,859
And everything on it came off the shelf.

713
00:50:56,354 --> 00:50:58,689
- Everything that was there.
- Right. Right.

714
00:50:59,120 --> 00:51:01,666
Was there anything not on the sheet?

715
00:51:02,537 --> 00:51:03,537
Like what?

716
00:51:04,203 --> 00:51:05,621
I am not saying like what.

717
00:51:06,329 --> 00:51:08,126
I found one more behind the coats.

718
00:51:11,745 --> 00:51:12,745
One more what?

719
00:51:16,173 --> 00:51:17,694
Forty-four umbrellas.

720
00:51:18,166 --> 00:51:20,814
The boy has been with me since ten o'clock.

721
00:51:21,644 --> 00:51:23,630
- Dot.
- I heard the tannoy, Gerald.

722
00:51:24,771 --> 00:51:28,218
- I am not accusing anybody.
- No. Nobody is.

723
00:51:38,031 --> 00:51:39,623
<i>Is that the four-floor one?</i>

724
00:51:40,636 --> 00:51:42,757
It is. It has been since ten.

725
00:51:46,898 --> 00:51:49,185
<i>Right. Give me twenty minutes.</i>

726
00:51:50,260 --> 00:51:51,883
Terry says twenty minutes.

727
00:51:55,354 --> 00:51:57,221
He said twenty minutes at eleven.

728
00:51:58,249 --> 00:52:01,899
- And at one o'clock.
- Four times. I have counted them.

729
00:52:02,973 --> 00:52:05,284
Then it all goes down the back stairs.

730
00:52:05,954 --> 00:52:08,133
- All of it?
- All of it. By hand.

731
00:52:16,714 --> 00:52:18,603
Nobody is accusing anybody.

732
00:52:19,235 --> 00:52:21,089
Nobody has accused anybody.

733
00:52:21,511 --> 00:52:25,943
- Then what are we doing?
- The first floor. All of it. Again.

734
00:52:26,863 --> 00:52:29,303
Soft Furnishings alone is four hours.

735
00:52:33,446 --> 00:52:36,166
Then we start now. Get the stock sheets.

736
00:52:37,043 --> 00:52:38,907
And the Lost Property sheet.

737
00:52:44,402 --> 00:52:46,123
And that one as well.

738
00:52:46,765 --> 00:52:51,272
- I can do that one again.
- You will do Bay Twelve with Dot.

739
00:52:52,413 --> 00:52:53,642
Why not Lost Property?

740
00:52:54,334 --> 00:52:56,611
Because I am doing Lost Property.

741
00:52:59,679 --> 00:53:01,595
Count it twice, say it once.

742
00:53:02,732 --> 00:53:03,873
Say what, exactly?

743
00:53:04,731 --> 00:53:06,714
- I did count it.
- I know you did.

744
00:53:07,428 --> 00:53:08,668
You said that to me.

745
00:53:09,782 --> 00:53:11,361
I said it to the room.

746
00:53:19,873 --> 00:53:21,592
- Kyle.
- I need some air.

747
00:53:22,040 --> 00:53:23,040
It is raining.

748
00:53:24,208 --> 00:53:25,745
<i>Door six has just gone up.</i>

749
00:53:26,395 --> 00:53:28,132
<i>Who is that on door six?</i>

750
00:53:28,737 --> 00:53:30,626
[rain on the loading bay roof]

751
00:53:38,523 --> 00:53:39,523
[radio static]

752
00:53:40,434 --> 00:53:42,784
<i>Ayres to floor. Third is clear.</i>

753
00:53:43,870 --> 00:53:46,244
- Received, Mrs. Ayres.
- <i>Righto.</i>

754
00:53:47,041 --> 00:53:48,349
That is four times, Dot.

755
00:53:48,858 --> 00:53:49,858
- Five.
- Five.

756
00:53:50,675 --> 00:53:52,518
Eleven short. The same eleven.

757
00:53:58,261 --> 00:54:01,818
Two lamps, a boxed kettle
and eight cards of curtain hooks.

758
00:54:06,062 --> 00:54:08,066
Count it twice, say it once.

759
00:54:11,582 --> 00:54:15,180
- I have said it nine times.
- Then it is not the saying.

760
00:54:15,776 --> 00:54:18,854
It is not the counting either.
I am good at counting.

761
00:54:25,034 --> 00:54:26,045
<i>Ayres to floor.</i>

762
00:54:26,593 --> 00:54:28,501
<i>Bay door is standing open again.</i>

763
00:54:29,365 --> 00:54:33,210
- She has said that all night.
- She has said it since ten.

764
00:54:34,124 --> 00:54:36,403
<i>That is the third time I said it.</i>

765
00:54:40,724 --> 00:54:43,378
- Does it matter?
- It has not stopped mattering.

766
00:54:44,231 --> 00:54:45,497
<i>Ayres to Haberdashery.</i>

767
00:54:46,441 --> 00:54:48,473
<i>Nobody touched it. It sits open.</i>

768
00:54:49,277 --> 00:54:51,331
- Since the delivery.
- What delivery?

769
00:54:51,845 --> 00:54:53,572
The one that came at half nine.

770
00:54:54,384 --> 00:54:56,123
I signed for it in the rain.

771
00:55:03,960 --> 00:55:05,030
[pencil stops]

772
00:55:05,970 --> 00:55:06,970
Dot.

773
00:55:07,844 --> 00:55:08,844
Say that again.

774
00:55:09,795 --> 00:55:11,878
- Say what again?
- The delivery.

775
00:55:17,622 --> 00:55:19,206
It came in at half nine.

776
00:55:19,908 --> 00:55:21,626
Kyle booked it in at Bay Twelve.

777
00:55:25,505 --> 00:55:26,840
Onto the delivery sheet.

778
00:55:27,902 --> 00:55:31,091
Then before ten we walked
the floor and counted it again.

779
00:55:32,041 --> 00:55:33,619
Count it twice, say it once.

780
00:55:34,456 --> 00:55:37,318
We counted it twice
and we said it twice.

781
00:55:45,187 --> 00:55:46,502
The eleven are not gone.

782
00:55:47,306 --> 00:55:49,362
They are on the paper twice.

783
00:55:50,520 --> 00:55:53,867
Booked in at the bay, then counted on the floor.

784
00:55:54,932 --> 00:55:57,079
- Two entries.
- One kettle.

785
00:55:57,636 --> 00:55:59,435
The shop is not eleven short.

786
00:55:59,837 --> 00:56:01,457
The paperwork is eleven long.

787
00:56:01,952 --> 00:56:04,189
- That is it, then.
- That is it.

788
00:56:05,060 --> 00:56:06,940
Forty-one years. A column of sums.

789
00:56:07,341 --> 00:56:08,373
It usually is.

790
00:56:18,577 --> 00:56:19,577
Gerald.

791
00:56:20,019 --> 00:56:22,535
- Do not.
- I have not said anything yet.

792
00:56:23,634 --> 00:56:25,977
I know what you are going to say.

793
00:56:26,621 --> 00:56:28,777
- Do you?
- You will say I was wrong.

794
00:56:29,740 --> 00:56:32,800
I was going to say the stock
is not missing.

795
00:56:33,708 --> 00:56:34,799
But yes. Also that.

796
00:56:35,372 --> 00:56:38,487
- Where is he?
- The loading bay. He will not come in.

797
00:56:38,914 --> 00:56:40,722
He has been out there an hour.

798
00:56:45,873 --> 00:56:48,138
Forty-four umbrellas and no coat.

799
00:56:54,613 --> 00:56:55,613
[tannoy chime]

800
00:56:56,804 --> 00:56:58,407
<i>Good morning. This is Gerald.</i>

801
00:57:01,239 --> 00:57:02,306
<i>It is ten to four.</i>

802
00:57:03,381 --> 00:57:06,934
<i>Earlier I said a thing about
a member of staff.</i>

803
00:57:07,493 --> 00:57:09,778
<i>It was not true and I said it
over this.</i>

804
00:57:10,669 --> 00:57:13,150
<i>The stock is not missing.
It was counted twice.</i>

805
00:57:13,735 --> 00:57:15,200
<i>Nobody took anything.</i>

806
00:57:16,246 --> 00:57:17,948
<i>Kyle, that is the whole of it.</i>

807
00:57:23,782 --> 00:57:26,333
<i>I am sorry.
Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

808
00:57:27,464 --> 00:57:28,539
[tannoy clicks off]

809
00:57:29,295 --> 00:57:33,084
That is the first useful thing
that speaker has ever done.

810
00:57:34,059 --> 00:57:36,521
- Thank you.
- It was not a compliment.

811
00:57:45,233 --> 00:57:48,958
- Mind the back stairs.
- I know about the third step.

812
00:57:49,881 --> 00:57:52,875
- He will not come in for me.
- He will if Dot asks him.

813
00:57:53,402 --> 00:57:56,780
- Why Dot?
- I have not accused him of anything.

814
00:58:02,922 --> 00:58:04,338
<i>Ayres to floor. Bay door.</i>

815
00:58:05,531 --> 00:58:07,268
<i>Still open. I have mentioned it.</i>

816
00:58:08,320 --> 00:58:12,238
- She has mentioned it.
- She has mentioned it a lot.

817
00:58:12,901 --> 00:58:16,640
She was right all night and nobody wrote it down.

818
00:58:17,450 --> 00:58:18,995
That is security for you.

819
00:58:19,957 --> 00:58:21,610
That is all of us, Gerald.

820
00:58:29,672 --> 00:58:30,742
You can come in now.

821
00:58:31,234 --> 00:58:33,340
- Can I.
- The count was wrong. Not you.

822
00:58:34,165 --> 00:58:38,426
Nobody took anything. The figures
were on the stock sheets twice.

823
00:58:39,185 --> 00:58:40,561
So I am not a thief.

824
00:58:41,640 --> 00:58:44,386
You were never a thief. You were an easy sentence.

825
00:58:45,040 --> 00:58:47,974
- Gerald says he is sorry.
- He said it on the tannoy.

826
00:58:53,667 --> 00:58:57,284
- Everyone is four people.
- Four people and Mrs. Ayres.

827
00:58:58,014 --> 00:59:00,008
I am not going to say it was fine.

828
00:59:04,477 --> 00:59:06,060
Nobody is asking you to.

829
00:59:06,492 --> 00:59:08,511
- I was wrong. Obviously.
- Yes.

830
00:59:11,093 --> 00:59:13,698
- Is that the apology?
- It is the shape of one.

831
00:59:14,843 --> 00:59:15,962
It will have to do.

832
00:59:19,555 --> 00:59:22,086
- Come in out of the wet.
- In a minute.

833
00:59:29,801 --> 00:59:31,641
There was one by the skip.

834
00:59:32,706 --> 00:59:35,861
- One what?
- Umbrella. Black. Broken spoke.

835
00:59:39,332 --> 00:59:41,228
- Whose?
- Nobody's. They never are.

836
00:59:41,689 --> 00:59:43,961
Lost Property shuts with the shop.

837
00:59:44,772 --> 00:59:47,074
Then it is not lost. It is ours.

838
00:59:47,600 --> 00:59:49,231
Put it with the others.

839
00:59:50,250 --> 00:59:53,242
- How many is that now?
- Say it properly.

840
00:59:54,210 --> 00:59:55,336
Forty-five umbrellas.

841
01:00:01,898 --> 01:00:03,523
<i>Winnie, are you on this floor?</i>

842
01:00:09,277 --> 01:00:11,456
Soft Furnishings, second landing.

843
01:00:12,019 --> 01:00:13,856
Did I hear the tannoy apologise?

844
01:00:18,157 --> 01:00:20,865
- You did.
- I thought I had dreamt it.

845
01:00:21,607 --> 01:00:23,666
The count balances. Nothing is gone.

846
01:00:24,596 --> 01:00:27,837
- Then where is it?
- In the building. Somewhere.

847
01:00:28,569 --> 01:00:30,845
That is not the same as nowhere.

848
01:00:31,759 --> 01:00:33,310
<i>Ayres to floor. Van outside.</i>

849
01:00:38,993 --> 01:00:41,166
- A van.
- At four in the morning.

850
01:00:41,752 --> 01:00:43,782
<i>Says he is from the lift people.</i>

851
01:00:44,764 --> 01:00:46,570
- He is not.
- He is, apparently.

852
01:00:57,340 --> 01:00:59,566
- Evening.
- It is ten past four.

853
01:01:00,474 --> 01:01:01,938
Terrible road, that one.

854
01:01:02,608 --> 01:01:04,343
You said that at half eleven.

855
01:01:09,494 --> 01:01:11,383
Terry says twenty minutes.

856
01:01:12,244 --> 01:01:14,667
- Who told you that?
- You did. All night.

857
01:01:17,841 --> 01:01:18,841
Sounds like me.

858
01:01:19,683 --> 01:01:22,370
- Are you not sorry?
- Not especially, no.

859
01:01:23,157 --> 01:01:26,608
- Is that a cup of tea?
- It is. Do you want one?

860
01:01:27,703 --> 01:01:29,131
After. Where is she, then?

861
01:01:41,923 --> 01:01:44,290
The goods lift has not moved since half ten.

862
01:01:45,047 --> 01:01:47,721
Between the mezzanine and one, apparently.

863
01:01:48,625 --> 01:01:51,497
- Is there a trick to it?
- There is a handle.

864
01:01:52,113 --> 01:01:54,188
{\an8}GOODS LIFT - SAFE LOAD 600KG

865
01:01:59,742 --> 01:02:00,742
Mind your feet.

866
01:02:01,437 --> 01:02:04,461
- Will anything fall out?
- Something always does.

867
01:02:05,615 --> 01:02:07,548
[lift doors grinding open]

868
01:02:08,340 --> 01:02:09,340
Oh.

869
01:02:10,415 --> 01:02:13,690
- Those are the reindeer.
- Those are the grotto reindeer.

870
01:02:14,520 --> 01:02:16,918
- And two lamps.
- And a boxed kettle.

871
01:02:19,581 --> 01:02:22,323
- And the curtain hooks.
- And the curtain hooks.

872
01:02:26,693 --> 01:02:28,117
Eleven items, all present.

873
01:02:29,189 --> 01:02:31,885
- Shall I shut her up again?
- Leave it.

874
01:02:32,480 --> 01:02:34,227
Leave it open a minute.

875
01:02:35,039 --> 01:02:37,573
- Nobody move.
- Nobody was going to.

876
01:02:45,660 --> 01:02:46,936
Bay Twelve. Go on.

877
01:02:47,986 --> 01:02:50,599
- Nine.
- It was eleven at two o'clock.

878
01:02:56,574 --> 01:03:00,214
It was eleven because you counted
the two on the trolley.

879
01:03:00,770 --> 01:03:04,426
- The trolley isn't Bay Twelve.
- The trolley is where it's standing.

880
01:03:05,428 --> 01:03:07,267
<i>Winnie. Are you receiving?</i>

881
01:03:08,183 --> 01:03:09,538
Receiving, Mrs. Ayres.

882
01:03:10,356 --> 01:03:14,314
<i>There is a man on the third floor</i>
<i>eating a sandwich.</i>

883
01:03:14,847 --> 01:03:15,847
That's Terry.

884
01:03:16,637 --> 01:03:17,850
<i>It isn't his sandwich.</i>

885
01:03:21,133 --> 01:03:22,596
[two-way radio crackles]

886
01:03:23,418 --> 01:03:25,246
Bay Twelve, Kyle. Go again.

887
01:03:25,727 --> 01:03:27,332
Nine. I have counted it twice.

888
01:03:28,407 --> 01:03:29,963
Count it twice, say it once.

889
01:03:30,640 --> 01:03:32,692
Nine. That's the floor done.

890
01:03:42,189 --> 01:03:44,634
- Where's Gerald?
- Where do you think.

891
01:03:49,974 --> 01:03:53,085
<i>Good morning, Pallister's.</i>
<i>The time is five nineteen.</i>

892
01:03:53,725 --> 01:03:56,972
<i>All stock sheets to the mezzanine</i>
<i>desk by half past, please.</i>

893
01:03:57,644 --> 01:03:59,068
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

894
01:04:03,144 --> 01:04:06,608
He has wanted to say "good morning"
since ten o'clock last night.

895
01:04:19,481 --> 01:04:21,555
Kyle. Lost Property. Under the shelf.

896
01:04:21,956 --> 01:04:25,208
- I've done Lost Property.
- You've done the shelf.

897
01:04:25,809 --> 01:04:28,133
There is always a box under a shelf.

898
01:04:28,644 --> 01:04:30,635
- What's in it?
- One umbrella.

899
01:04:31,779 --> 01:04:34,624
Green. Two spokes gone.
A name inside the strap.

900
01:04:35,211 --> 01:04:36,211
Whose?

901
01:04:37,227 --> 01:04:38,227
"D. Hollis."

902
01:04:42,567 --> 01:04:44,698
No Hollis has ever worked here.

903
01:04:45,293 --> 01:04:47,260
Then he lost it fair and square.

904
01:04:49,980 --> 01:04:53,353
Why does a town by the sea
lose this many umbrellas?

905
01:04:59,411 --> 01:05:01,262
- Wind.
- That is not an answer.

906
01:05:06,661 --> 01:05:07,843
It's the wind, Kyle.

907
01:05:08,250 --> 01:05:11,742
People put them down. Then they
stop thinking about them.

908
01:05:12,245 --> 01:05:14,124
Forty-one years I have wondered.

909
01:05:14,961 --> 01:05:16,415
- And?
- And it's the wind.

910
01:05:17,032 --> 01:05:18,201
Forty-six umbrellas.

911
01:05:23,995 --> 01:05:25,391
<i>Winnie! Come down here!</i>

912
01:05:29,777 --> 01:05:31,599
<i>Terry's done it! It's going!</i>

913
01:05:37,481 --> 01:05:40,398
- Come down where?
- The back stairs. Now.

914
01:05:41,403 --> 01:05:43,063
[goods lift motor turns over]

915
01:05:43,597 --> 01:05:44,597
It's going.

916
01:05:45,023 --> 01:05:47,520
It has been going since ten to five.

917
01:05:48,698 --> 01:05:49,774
You said four.

918
01:05:50,319 --> 01:05:51,387
I said about four.

919
01:05:52,552 --> 01:05:54,493
Ten to five is not about four.

920
01:06:00,437 --> 01:06:02,698
A thing had come off another thing.

921
01:06:11,679 --> 01:06:14,262
Right. The grotto. What goes first?

922
01:06:15,158 --> 01:06:19,434
- Nothing. We count, we don't clear.
- The reindeer's counted.

923
01:06:19,860 --> 01:06:22,405
The reindeer has been counted twice.

924
01:06:23,088 --> 01:06:24,709
Then the reindeer can go down.

925
01:06:25,350 --> 01:06:27,418
- It won't fit.
- It will on its side.

926
01:06:27,963 --> 01:06:30,517
It has never once gone on its side.

927
01:06:31,248 --> 01:06:33,503
It went up on its side in 1986.

928
01:06:34,284 --> 01:06:35,973
Legs first. Head at the back.

929
01:06:36,944 --> 01:06:39,719
- Mind its ear.
- It hasn't got an ear.

930
01:06:44,871 --> 01:06:46,354
It had one when it went up.

931
01:06:53,898 --> 01:06:55,822
Send it down to the loading bay.

932
01:06:58,888 --> 01:07:00,149
Loading bay it is.

933
01:07:00,646 --> 01:07:03,944
<i>Security to all floors.</i>
<i>The goods lift is moving.</i>

934
01:07:07,612 --> 01:07:09,101
We know. We put it in there.

935
01:07:10,116 --> 01:07:11,503
<i>Nobody tells me anything.</i>

936
01:07:12,016 --> 01:07:13,470
<i>Also my sandwich has gone.</i>

937
01:07:13,913 --> 01:07:15,635
<i>Don't look into it. I know.</i>

938
01:07:25,736 --> 01:07:26,736
Door up.

939
01:07:27,829 --> 01:07:28,829
[gulls]

940
01:07:29,970 --> 01:07:32,465
- It's light.
- Since about ten minutes ago.

941
01:07:32,910 --> 01:07:34,407
That's the sea, that is.

942
01:07:35,114 --> 01:07:37,121
I know what it is. I live here.

943
01:07:42,955 --> 01:07:44,542
Put it by the skip. Not in it.

944
01:07:45,477 --> 01:07:46,477
By the skip.

945
01:07:47,162 --> 01:07:51,126
- Somebody will want it.
- Nobody has ever wanted it.

946
01:07:51,874 --> 01:07:52,874
Somebody will.

947
01:08:00,743 --> 01:08:03,673
You've been sat in Soft Furnishings
since half two.

948
01:08:04,493 --> 01:08:06,900
I was waiting for the count to finish.

949
01:08:07,428 --> 01:08:08,655
It's finished, Fenella.

950
01:08:12,950 --> 01:08:14,411
Then I'll tell you.

951
01:08:15,387 --> 01:08:17,289
The sale completes on the sixth.

952
01:08:18,128 --> 01:08:21,531
The buyer wants the ground floor.
The rest goes to offices.

953
01:08:22,728 --> 01:08:23,728
And the staff.

954
01:08:24,344 --> 01:08:27,911
Twelve of the jobs cross over with the building.

955
01:08:28,386 --> 01:08:30,142
Thirty-one people work here.

956
01:08:31,129 --> 01:08:32,129
Yes.

957
01:08:32,898 --> 01:08:36,218
- Which twelve?
- I don't pick them. The buyer does.

958
01:08:37,411 --> 01:08:38,482
When do they hear?

959
01:08:39,434 --> 01:08:40,805
Letters on the fourteenth.

960
01:08:41,964 --> 01:08:43,609
- That's nine days.
- Yes.

961
01:08:44,033 --> 01:08:45,467
And the rest of them?

962
01:08:46,448 --> 01:08:50,031
Statutory, plus a week a year,
capped at twenty years.

963
01:08:55,515 --> 01:08:56,784
Dot has done forty-one.

964
01:08:58,893 --> 01:09:01,757
I know what Dot has done.
The cap is still twenty.

965
01:09:05,367 --> 01:09:09,585
Is there a version of this
where I tell them it's fine?

966
01:09:10,576 --> 01:09:14,611
No. It's twelve jobs and a cap.
That is the whole of it.

967
01:09:15,288 --> 01:09:16,288
And me?

968
01:09:17,260 --> 01:09:21,061
There's a job going at Coombe Head if you want it.

969
01:09:21,947 --> 01:09:24,950
- Coombe Head is a shed.
- It's a smaller building.

970
01:09:36,985 --> 01:09:39,009
Kyle. Haberdashery. Two minutes.

971
01:09:39,800 --> 01:09:44,477
- I've counted Haberdashery.
- I'm not asking you to count it.

972
01:09:44,979 --> 01:09:46,026
Give me the coat.

973
01:09:51,648 --> 01:09:54,990
- It has to go back.
- The coat does. The tape doesn't.

974
01:09:55,769 --> 01:09:57,358
Stand still. Arms down.

975
01:10:01,487 --> 01:10:03,492
- Is it straight?
- It's straight.

976
01:10:07,757 --> 01:10:10,632
There. Inside the collar
of your own jacket.

977
01:10:11,722 --> 01:10:13,459
Nobody will ever see it.

978
01:10:13,975 --> 01:10:15,726
- No.
- That is rather the point.

979
01:10:16,465 --> 01:10:17,465
Thanks, Dot.

980
01:10:27,539 --> 01:10:29,362
That's the stock sheets done.

981
01:10:29,872 --> 01:10:31,463
- How many?
- Forty-one.

982
01:10:32,262 --> 01:10:33,442
Sign the top one.

983
01:10:34,568 --> 01:10:36,435
{\an8}STOCK SHEET 41 OF 41 - CHECKED

984
01:10:42,492 --> 01:10:44,353
<i>Stock-take signed off, is it?</i>

985
01:10:44,966 --> 01:10:46,277
Signed off, Mrs. Ayres.

986
01:10:58,611 --> 01:11:00,193
Everyone out the front.

987
01:11:01,296 --> 01:11:04,820
- Where's Gerald?
- On the tannoy. Where else.

988
01:11:05,265 --> 01:11:06,792
<i>Good morning, Pallister's.</i>

989
01:11:07,317 --> 01:11:10,847
<i>The stock-take is complete.</i>
<i>Four of you did it.</i>

990
01:11:11,815 --> 01:11:13,096
<i>Thank you, Pallister's.</i>

991
01:11:14,013 --> 01:11:15,313
[tannoy clicks off]

992
01:11:26,676 --> 01:11:28,365
[shutters rattling upward]

993
01:11:33,579 --> 01:11:37,759
- That's the last time but one.
- Last time but two. Tuesday, then the keys.

994
01:11:43,924 --> 01:11:45,301
Sun's up over Marchbank.

995
01:11:46,424 --> 01:11:47,928
<i>Front shutters up. Logged.</i>

996
01:11:52,722 --> 01:11:55,622
- Does she ever come out?
- Not in nine years.

997
01:11:59,155 --> 01:12:01,279
Right. Home, the lot of you.

998
01:12:02,069 --> 01:12:05,294
- What do I do Saturday?
- Come in at nine.

999
01:12:08,741 --> 01:12:10,300
Count it twice, say it once.

1000
01:12:21,652 --> 01:12:24,517
{\an8}PALLISTER AND DUNN - MARCHBANK - 1911 TO 2015

